{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1785391888,"nonce":"ZR5mKzjRel0vrGocVmdAAWu5rEk=","message":"**Amended 2026-07-30** — the loudness criterion was rewritten and two criteria added. ADR-0063 parts 5 and 6 moved with it, and the rejected alternative is recorded there.\n\nThe original criterion said *\"an Attempt whose logs were **evicted** says so\"*. Read literally that is \"consult the eviction record\", and an implementer who does exactly that will, on finding no row, conclude nothing happened and stream whatever is on disk. That fails on the case the slice exists to close: a volume that was lost or reprovisioned was evicted by nobody, so there is no row, and the reader gets a blank pane.\n\nNor is that case reachable by detecting the loss. **A volume's loss is not observable as an event** — a fresh empty volume is indistinguishable from one that never held data. Absence is the only signal there is, so it has to be the authoritative one, and the eviction record demotes to an explanation.\n\nTwo consequences, both now criteria:\n\n1. The three-branch read path, stated explicitly so it cannot be built record-first, plus a test where the bytes vanish with **no** eviction record. That test is the point: a record-first implementation passes every test in which eviction actually happened.\n2. A boot-time volume-identity marker (part 6). Per-Run absences are each individually correct and collectively uninformative; the operator needs one line saying the storage is not the storage the index describes.\n\n**Rejected in the same discussion:** stamping per-Attempt log durability, the way ADR-0064 part 5 stamps snapshot durability. The asymmetry is deliberate — 0064 stamps a re-derivable class, where \"never archived\" tells a reader which Runs are worth re-running; a log has no recompute path, so the stamp separates two states with the same available action. Durability is also the operator's cost decision, which is why warm-only deployments are supported at all. Worth knowing that the ingredient exists anyway: part 8's eviction pin already needs per-chunk sink-acknowledgement state, so if this is ever reopened it is a surfacing decision, not a plumbing one.","files":null}]}